INT. GRAND HOTEL ICE-SCULPTURE WORKSHOP - NIGHT

A walk-in freezer converted to a studio. Frost on every surface. A half-carved swan stands on a plinth, a chainsaw hangs from a hook, and a work lamp throws a cold blue circle on the floor.

FELICITY AMADI-STRÖM (30s, parka, ski goggles) crouches at the base of the swan, a chisel in her glove. BARNABAS OKONKWO-REYES (40s, wool hat, breath fogging) holds the lamp steady. They work in silence, whispering only when necessary.

FELICITY
Left a bit.

BARNABAS
This is left.

FELICITY
Your left.

BARNABAS
It's my left.

FELICITY
Mine, then.

He sighs. Shifts the lamp. The beam slides across the ice, and inside the swan's translucent belly, a dark shape appears.

FELICITY (CONT'D)
There.

BARNABAS
You're sure?

FELICITY
Fifteen pounds of platinum. Frozen in solid.

BARNABAS
Why would anyone put it in a swan?

FELICITY
Because no one chisels a swan.

BARNABAS
We're chiseling a swan.

FELICITY
We're not anyone.

She taps the ice with the chisel. A crack travels along the neck. Barnabas winces.

BARNABAS
Careful.

FELICITY
It's only a swan.

BARNABAS
It's a swan worth four thousand dollars, and the gala is tomorrow.

FELICITY
Then we'll leave them a duck.

The freezer door clanks. They freeze.

A figure enters. DESMOND ACHEBE-LINDGREN (50s, executive chef, whites under a long coat) stops just inside, studying the swan, then them.

DESMOND
Evening.

FELICITY
Evening, Chef.

BARNABAS
We were just finishing.

DESMOND
At two a.m.?

FELICITY
Inspiration.

DESMOND
It is a very good swan.

BARNABAS
Thank you.

DESMOND
It would be a better swan with its belly intact.

A pause. Desmond crosses to the chainsaw and lifts it off the hook, casually, like a man picking up a ladle.

FELICITY
Chef, we can explain.

DESMOND
I would hope so.

BARNABAS
It was Felicity's idea.

FELICITY
It was not!

DESMOND
It was mine, actually.

They stare at him.

DESMOND (CONT'D)
I froze the platinum in. Sixteen years ago. You are both very late.

He smiles, a long, slow, wolfish smile, and pulls the cord. The chainsaw roars. Felicity and Barnabas leap backward, and the swan, quivering, begins to crack from the neck down.
